{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DMr3y3p8HDQDJuXnVhLdR1","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"ACL Rolling Review review form soundness excitement overall score scale 1-5\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ACL 2023 Acceptance Recommendations - ACL 2023\",\"url\":\"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/overall-recommendation/\"},{\"title\":\"Program Chairs’ Report on Peer Review at ACL 2023 Anna Rogers}\",\"url\":\"https://aclanthology.org/2023.acl-long.report.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines\"},{\"title\":\"ACL 2026 on X: \\\"Checkout the blog post on the soundness/excitement scores https://t.co/AKSO9DjiYP . It clarifies the reasoning behind the separation of overall scores and how it might make the reviewer recommendation more robust. #ACL2023Toronto #ACL2023NLP #NLProc\\\" / X\",\"url\":\"https://x.com/aclmeeting/status/1637827267173953540\"},{\"title\":\"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/\"},{\"title\":\"Program Chairs' Report on Peer Review at ACL 2023\",\"url\":\"http://www.cs.umd.edu/~jbg/docs/2023_acl_peer_review_report.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md\"},{\"title\":\"ACL’23 Peer Review Policies - ACL 2023\",\"url\":\"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/review-acl23/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's information about the ACL Rolling Review form's soundness, excitement, and overall score scales:\n\n## Scoring Structure\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n### Soundness Score\n\nThe soundness scale includes: 5 = Excellent (this study is one of the most thorough), 4 = Strong (provides sufficient support for all claims), and 3 = Acceptable (provides sufficient support for main claims). The assessment considers whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, clearly states scientific claims with adequate support, and for experimental papers evaluates the depth/breadth of research questions, technical soundness of experiments, and methodological validity.\n\n### Excitement Score\n\nThe excitement scale includes: 5 = Highly Exciting (I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation) and 4 = Exciting (I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation). At the low end, 1 = Not Exciting (the paper does not resonate with the reviewer and wouldn't with the *ACL community). Excitement is subjective and may be based on papers perceived as transformational, innovative, or surprising due to conceptual breakthroughs or evidence challenging common assumptions.\n\n### Overall Recommendation Score\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of the paper, reflecting an assessment of soundness, excitement, and other factors like novelty and impact.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b278507-b3d6-452e-b8e4-ad54eb721597","uuid":"81d1a3ae-d504-4903-8666-38e3db515435","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:48:52.008Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"ACL Rolling Review review form soundness excitement overall score scale 1-5","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01FesD6G5k9H59gJzdgQtDHh","content":[{"title":"Review Form – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"},{"title":"ARR Reviewer Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewerguidelines"},{"title":"ACL 2023 Acceptance Recommendations - ACL 2023","url":"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/overall-recommendation/"},{"title":"Program Chairs’ Report on Peer Review at ACL 2023 Anna Rogers}","url":"https://aclanthology.org/2023.acl-long.report.pdf"},{"title":"ARR Area Chair Guidelines – ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/acguidelines"},{"title":"ACL 2026 on X: \"Checkout the blog post on the soundness/excitement scores https://t.co/AKSO9DjiYP . It clarifies the reasoning behind the separation of overall scores and how it might make the reviewer recommendation more robust. #ACL2023Toronto #ACL2023NLP #NLProc\" / X","url":"https://x.com/aclmeeting/status/1637827267173953540"},{"title":"ACL Rolling Review – A peer review platform for the Association for Computational Linguistics","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/"},{"title":"Program Chairs' Report on Peer Review at ACL 2023","url":"http://www.cs.umd.edu/~jbg/docs/2023_acl_peer_review_report.pdf"},{"title":"aclrollingreview/reviewerguidelines.md at main · acl-org/aclrollingreview","url":"https://github.com/acl-org/aclrollingreview/blob/main/reviewerguidelines.md"},{"title":"ACL’23 Peer Review Policies - ACL 2023","url":"https://2023.aclweb.org/blog/review-acl23/"}]},"Based on the search results, here's information about the ACL Rolling Review form's soundness, excitement, and overall score scales:\n\n## Scoring Structure\n\nStarting with February 2025, ARR reviewers are asked to provide three scores: soundness, excitement, and overall recommendation.\n\n### Soundness Score\n\nThe soundness scale includes: 5 = Excellent (this study is one of the most thorough), 4 = Strong (provides sufficient support for all claims), and 3 = Acceptable (provides sufficient support for main claims). The assessment considers whether the paper is sufficiently sound and thorough, clearly states scientific claims with adequate support, and for experimental papers evaluates the depth/breadth of research questions, technical soundness of experiments, and methodological validity.\n\n### Excitement Score\n\nThe excitement scale includes: 5 = Highly Exciting (I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation) and 4 = Exciting (I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation). At the low end, 1 = Not Exciting (the paper does not resonate with the reviewer and wouldn't with the *ACL community). Excitement is subjective and may be based on papers perceived as transformational, innovative, or surprising due to conceptual breakthroughs or evidence challenging common assumptions.\n\n### Overall Recommendation Score\n\nThe Overall assessment score is an explicit recommendation for the outcome of the paper, reflecting an assessment of soundness, excitement, and other factors like novelty and impact."],"durationSeconds":6.665504675999997}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01HVc2EzmWxHnBN1JiQazx9S","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the ARR review form structure. Let me get the exact scoring scales.\n\nBased on my research, the ARR review form (used for EMNLP 2026) has:\n\n**Soundness**: 1-5 scale\n- 5 = Excellent\n- 4 = Strong\n- 3 = Acceptable\n- 2 = Poor\n- 1 = Major Issues\n\n**Excitement**: 1-5 scale\n- 5 = Highly Exciting\n- 4 = Exciting\n- 3 = Ambivalent\n\n\n- 2 = Mediocre\n- 1 = Not Exciting\n\n**Overall Assessment**: 1-5 scale\n- 5 = Strong Accept\n- 4 = Accept\n- 3 = Borderline\n- 2 = Reject\n- 1 = Strong Reject\n\nWith EMNLP 2025's ~22% acceptance rate in mind, I'm ready to dispatch the reviews—R1 will handle methods and experiments while R2 covers novelty and related work, then we'll move through the full roundtable discussion.\n\nI need to structure this as four steps: R1 and R2 review independently in parallel, then cross-share their findings, converge on a shared assessment, and finally synthesize everything into a comprehensive report. Let me set up both reviewers as parallel agents with the full paper content and review guidelines.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1112,"cache_read_input_tokens":59680,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1112},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3b278507-b3d6-452e-b8e4-ad54eb721597","uuid":"43a9e1e3-4901-4ce4-8d7b-569021923020"}
